Mock-forces-branch is UNVERIFIED — this test's generated test bodies are empty, so it was passing vacuously
=========================================================================

PARKED 2026-08-06. Not a feature gap and not a known bug — an open question
about whose spelling is wrong. Marked TODO so it stops reporting green while
asserting nothing; deleting this marker is a human decision.

WHAT WAS MEASURED

`post.sh` runs `zig test output_emitted.zig` and reported "All 3 tests passed",
which is what made this look green. The generated blocks it was passing are
these, verbatim from `output_emitted.zig`:

    test "Withdrawal succeeds when mocked" {
    }
    test "Withdrawal fails when funds insufficient" {
    }

Empty. The whole artifact contains ZERO `assert` calls, so nothing in this test
constrains mocking, branch pruning, or the mocked value 50. The test names are
correct and the block count is correct, which is exactly why the vacuity
survived: the only thing wrong is that the bodies are missing.

THE MECHANISM

A `test(...)` body is re-parsed under the synthetic filename `__test__.kz`
(koru_std/testing.kz:280, src/compiler.zig's Parser.init call at
koru_std/compiler.kz:1460). Under a `.kz` filename a Koru construct must open
with `~` — that is the host→Koru switch. This file is a `.k`, where everything is
Koru and nothing carries `~`, so every line of the body parses as a Zig host line
instead: zero mocks collected, zero flows collected, empty block emitted.

Cross-check that pins it down rather than guessing: 395_008_complex_flow_test is
a `.kz` whose body IS `~`-prefixed, and it emits a real body. So a working
spelling exists; what is unresolved is whether THIS spelling should also work.

THE QUESTION, WHICH IS NOT MINE

Two readings, and they lead to opposite fixes:

  (a) The TEST is wrong. A test body is a nested Koru context spelled the `.kz`
      way, so this file should either become a `.kz` with `~`-prefixed body, or
      keep `.k` and add the prefixes. Cheap, local, and makes the test assert
      what its name claims.

  (b) The PARSER is wrong. In a `.k` file every construct is Koru by definition;
      requiring `~` inside a test body — because the body is handed to the parser
      under a synthetic `.kz` name — leaks an implementation detail of test
      re-parsing into the user's syntax. Under this reading the synthetic
      filename should inherit the entry file's dialect, and both of these tests
      are spelled correctly today.

Reading (b) is the one that would change the language, so it needs a ruling, and
the ruling has not been made. Lars looked at these on 2026-08-06 and read them as
"fine as written", which is consistent with (b) and is not yet a decision.

WHEN RESOLVED

Under (a): fix the spelling, delete this marker, and the test starts asserting
its own name. Under (b): fix the dialect inheritance, delete this marker, and
this test becomes the regression that proves it. Either way the acceptance
criterion is the same and is worth stating so nobody settles for less: the
emitted `test` blocks must be NON-EMPTY and the artifact must contain the
`assert` calls this file writes.

Related: 395_009_cross_module_mock is parked for the identical reason.
`--todo-sweep` will run both anyway, which is how a silent fix gets noticed.
